# Everything the service Dockerfiles need is source: the .csproj files,
# global.json, the Directory.Build.props files, then src/ and libs/. Nothing
# listed below is any of that, and this file is load-bearing three times over.
#
# Context size. Without it the context is the entire working tree plus
# whatever the job just produced. The .NET jobs run restore, build and test on
# the host before building the image, so bin/ and obj/ are already populated
# and large by the time docker sees them -- and .claude/ holds git worktrees.
# That context was the real reason `docker build` took 633s for atlas: the
# legacy builder tars and ships the whole thing to the daemon before running a
# single instruction. BuildKit only transfers what a COPY actually references,
# which is most of why that step is now measured in tens of seconds.
#
# Correctness. `COPY src/<svc>/ src/<svc>/` lands after the container has run
# its own `dotnet restore`, so a host obj/ overwrites the restore output that
# the following `dotnet publish --no-restore` depends on -- including a
# project.assets.json carrying NuGet paths from the host, which do not exist
# inside the image. It has worked so far because the host restored the same
# packages. Excluding obj/ makes the container's own restore the only one
# present, which is what --no-restore was always assuming.
#
# Caching. A COPY whose contents change on every run can never be a cache hit.
# Host bin/ and obj/ change on every run by definition, so without this the
# restore layer would cache and every layer after it -- publish included --
# would miss, every time.

**/bin/
**/obj/
**/node_modules/
**/TestResults/
**/.next/
**/dist/
**/coverage/

.git/
.github/
.claude/
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
docs/

**/*.user
**/.DS_Store

# Development overrides must never reach a deployed artifact. These are not in
# any image today only by accident: the host's stale obj/ used to make
# `dotnet publish --no-restore` skip copying content files, so excluding obj/
# above is what started publishing them. Trivy caught it immediately, on
# atlas's appsettings.Development.json, which carries Azurite's published
# devstoreaccount1 connection string -- a public constant, so nothing leaked,
# but a Development config inside a production image would override real
# settings the moment DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT said Development.
#
# appsettings.json itself is deliberately NOT excluded. That one is the
# deployed configuration and the image needs it.
**/appsettings.Development.json
